/// Chapter 22 of the guide, written from md-docs/about/accessibility.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page AccessibilityGuide(path: "/docs/guide/accessibility", title: "Accessibility", description: "What the built-ins do for keyboard and screen-reader users, the A01–A17 checks, what is still yours to do, and how to test it.", layout: DocsShell("Accessibility", kind: .explanation, chapter: t("nav.group.ui"), blurb: "Build pages that work with a keyboard, a screen reader or voice control: what the built-ins do, what the compiler checks, and what is still yours to do.", source: "md-docs/about/accessibility.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "know-what-the-built-ins-do-for-you", label: "Know what the built-ins do for you"), Anchor(id: "fix-what-the-compiler-reports", label: "Fix what the compiler reports"), Anchor(id: "do-what-the-compiler-cannot-check", label: "Do what the compiler cannot check"), Anchor(id: "label-controls-well", label: "Label controls well"), Anchor(id: "test-with-assistive-technology", label: "Test with assistive technology"), Anchor(id: "checklist", label: "Checklist")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/media", prevLabel: t("ch.21"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/forms", nextLabel: t("ch.23"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to build pages that people can use with a keyboard, a screen reader, voice control, zoom or reduced motion. Most of the work is done for you: the built-ins render the markup assistive technology expects, the router and forms manage focus, and the compiler checks every page on every build. What is left — meaning, wording, order — is yours, and this chapter lists it.\n\nIn this chapter you will:\n\n- learn what the built-ins already do, so you do not redo it;\n- fix what the compiler reports;\n- do the work no compiler can check;\n- label controls well;\n- test a page the way its readers use it.")

        Heading("Know what the built-ins do for you", id: "know-what-the-built-ins-do-for-you").h2

        Markdown("You get these behaviours without asking, as long as you use the built-in elements rather than rebuilding them:\n\n- **Semantic elements.** A `Button` is a `<button>`, a `Link` an `<a>`, `Heading(…).h2` an `<h2>`. `Header`, `Section` and `Footer` are landmarks. A `Table` is a `<table>` whose head cells are `<th scope=\"col\">`, a `List` is a `<ul>` or `<ol>`, and the page's content is a `<main>`.\n- **A skip link.** It is first in the tab order, visible when focused, and jumps past the navigation to the content.\n- **Route changes that behave like page loads.** Focus moves to the new page's heading, the scroll returns to the top, and the new title is announced.\n- **Labelled fields.** `label:` is a real `<label for>`. `hint:` and the error are tied to the control with `aria-describedby`. An error sets `aria-invalid` and is announced when it appears ([Forms](/docs/guide/forms)).\n- **Forms that point at the problem.** A submit that fails moves focus to the first field with an error and announces it.\n- **Dialogs that hold focus.** `Modal` and `Dialog` are real `<dialog>` elements: the rest of the page is inert, Escape closes them, and focus returns to what opened them.\n- **Focus held where you ask.** Any other element holds focus with `trapFocus: open` — a find bar, a panel. Focus moves in, Tab and Shift+Tab go round inside it, a click outside brings focus back, and when `open` turns false, focus returns to where it was.\n- **Menus with the keys people expect.** The arrow keys move and skip a disabled item, Home and End jump to the ends, Enter and Space choose, ArrowRight opens a submenu and ArrowLeft leaves it, and Escape closes the innermost menu and returns focus. A check item is a `menuitemcheckbox` with `aria-checked`; an item's shortcut is its `aria-keyshortcuts`.\n- **Splitters you can move without a mouse.** A `Splitter` is a `separator` with its size as `aria-valuenow`. It takes focus, the arrow keys move it, Home and End go to its ends, and Enter resets it.\n- **Announcements.** A `Toast` is spoken politely. To announce anything else, call `WF.announce(text)`.\n- **The active link.** The link to the current page carries `aria-current=\"page\"`.\n- **Reduced motion.** A reader who asked for it gets no animation at all, and a carousel does not play on its own ([Motion](/docs/guide/motion#respect-reduced-motion)).\n- **Visible focus.** Every interactive built-in shows a focus ring for the keyboard (`:focus-visible`), but not on every click.")

        Heading("Fix what the compiler reports", id: "fix-what-the-compiler-reports").h2

        Markdown("The compiler checks every page for the mistakes it can see in the markup, and reports each as a warning on every build and in the editor. Each code has a full entry, with a fix, in [Diagnostics](/errors#accessibility), and `wf explain CODE` prints it in your terminal.")

        Table(caption: "Fix what the compiler reports") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Code").header
                    Table.Cell("What it checks").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A01`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Image` has `alt:` (an empty one for a decorative picture)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A02`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `IconButton` has a `label:`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A03`, `A04`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Input`, `Checkbox`, `Radio`, `Switch`, `Slider` and `Textarea` has a label") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A05`, `A06`, `A07`") }
                    Table.Cell("Buttons, links and headings have text")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A08`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Modal` and `Dialog` has a `title:`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A09`") }
                    Table.Cell("A video has captions and controls; audio has a transcript")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A10`") }
                    Table.Cell("A table has a header row")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A11`, `A12`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("No heading level is skipped, and each page has exactly one `h1`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A13`") }
                    Table.Cell("The theme's text and background colours meet WCAG AA contrast")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A14`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `role:` holds children of the roles it requires") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A15`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A control's `aria-label` contains its visible text, for voice control") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A16`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("No literal `id:` is shared by several elements, in a loop or a reused component") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A17`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Every `Frame` has a `title:`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("For example, this button's `aria-label` does not contain the word it shows, so a voice-control user who says \"click Save\" may not reach it:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Editor(path: "/", title: "Editor", description: "Write a note.") {
    Heading("Editor").h1
    Button("Save", aria-label: "Submit the form")
}"#, title: "wf · draws A15")

        Markdown("The fix is to start the `aria-label` with the visible words: `aria-label: \"Save the note\"`.")

        Heading("Do what the compiler cannot check", id: "do-what-the-compiler-cannot-check").h2

        Markdown("The compiler can see markup; it cannot see meaning. These are still yours:\n\n- **Alt text that says what the picture shows**, in its context. \"Photo\" passes `A01` and helps nobody.\n- **Link text that says where it goes.** Twelve \"Read more\" links pass `A06`.\n- **An order that makes sense.** The reading order is the source order. Do not rearrange it visually with `order:` or absolute positioning.\n- **Colour that is not the only signal.** An error in red also says so in words; a chart has labels.\n- **The contrast of your own styles.** `A13` checks the theme's tokens, not a colour written directly in a `style \{ \}` block.\n- **Custom controls.** A `Card` made clickable with `on click` is not a button to a keyboard ([Make a custom control work from the keyboard](#make-a-custom-control-work-from-the-keyboard)).\n- **Time limits.** A `Toast` disappears. Anything the reader must act on belongs in an `Alert` that stays.")

        Heading("Make a custom control work from the keyboard", id: "make-a-custom-control-work-from-the-keyboard").h3

        Markdown("Use `Button` whenever you can. When an element must act as a button, give it the role, a place in the tab order, and handlers for the keys a button answers to:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Plans(path: "/plans", title: "Plans", description: "Choose a plan.") {
    state chosen = ""
    Heading("Plans").h1
    Card(role: "button", tabindex: "0") {
        on click { chosen = "Team" }
        on key("Enter") { chosen = "Team" }
        on key("space") { chosen = "Team" }
        Text("Team plan")
    }
    Text("Chosen: {chosen}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Without `tabindex`, the card cannot be reached with Tab; without the key handlers, reaching it does nothing.")

        Heading("Label controls well", id: "label-controls-well").h2

        Markdown("Every control needs a name that assistive technology can read, and that matches what a sighted reader sees:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Search(path: "/", title: "Search", description: "Find a page.") {
    state q = ""
    Heading("Search").h1
    Input(bind: q, label: "Search the docs", hint: "Try “routing” or “forms”").search
    IconButton(icon: "close", label: "Clear the search") { on click { q = "" } }
    Button("Search", aria-label: "Search the docs") { on click { log(q) } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **Prefer a visible `label:` to a placeholder.** A placeholder disappears as soon as the reader types.\n- **Name an icon-only control.** Use `label:` on an `IconButton`, or `aria-label:` on anything else. The name is read aloud, never shown.\n- **Begin an `aria-label` with the visible words.** Someone who says \"click Search\" then reaches the right button (`A15`).")

        Heading("Test with assistive technology", id: "test-with-assistive-technology").h2

        Markdown("The compiler's checks are a floor. Before you ship a page, use it the way its readers do:\n\n1. **Use only the keyboard.** Put the mouse away. Tab through the page, open and close every menu and dialog, and submit every form. Focus is always visible and never lost.\n2. **Use a screen reader.** VoiceOver (macOS: ⌘F5), NVDA (Windows, free) or TalkBack (Android). Listen to the page's list of headings and landmarks, and to what a form says when it fails.\n3. **Zoom to 200%**, and narrow the window. Nothing needs horizontal scrolling.\n4. **Turn on reduced motion** in your system settings, and reload. Nothing moves.\n\nYou can also automate part of this. `wf test`'s `click \"Save\"` and `type \"…\" into \"Email\"` find controls by the name a reader sees, the way assistive technology does, so a test that passes is a page someone can operate ([Testing](/docs/guide/testing)).")

        Heading("Checklist", id: "checklist").h2

        Markdown("- `wf check` reports no `A` warnings.\n- Every picture's alt text says what it shows, and decorative ones have `alt: \"\"`.\n- Every link and button says what it does without the text around it.\n- Every control has a visible label, and every `aria-label` contains the visible words.\n- Colour is never the only way something is said.\n- Every custom control is reachable with Tab and works with Enter and Space.\n- The page works with only a keyboard, with a screen reader, at 200% zoom, and with reduced motion.")
    }
}
